<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">British Guiana</h1><p class="st-meta">1831 CE – 1966 CE · capital Georgetown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's colony on the South American mainland, a sugar colony whose Indian, African, Portuguese and Chinese communities shaped modern Guyana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1831 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> united the colonies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demerara-essequibo">Demerara-Essequibo</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/berbice">Berbice</a> into British Guiana, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Georgetown</a> as capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished in 1834, and after the apprenticeship period ended in 1838 most freed people left the plantations to found their own villages.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Planters replaced them with indentured labourers, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1838, as well as Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and Chinese workers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> remained dominant, with bauxite mining growing in the 20th century. An international tribunal in 1899 settled the western border with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. In 1953 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-progressive-party-guyana">People's Progressive Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheddi-jagan">Cheddi Jagan</a> won elections, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> suspended the constitution after 133 days. Politics then split between Jagan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham">Forbes Burnham</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Racial violence between Indian and African Guyanese followed in the early 1960s. British Guiana gained independence on 26 May 1966 as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/co-operative-republic-of-guyana">Guyana</a>, later the Co-operative Republic of Guyana, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham">Burnham</a> as prime minister.</p></div>